1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,815
You have an idea: help a business route support messages to the right team.

2
00:00:05,915 --> 00:00:07,892
But where do you begin?

3
00:00:07,992 --> 00:00:16,616
Bittensor has its own language, and building a useful service means connecting an interface, data, evaluation, running code and network tools.

4
00:00:16,716 --> 00:00:19,523
Those steps can feel like separate worlds.

5
00:00:19,623 --> 00:00:24,092
Think of a subnet as a team with a specific job.

6
00:00:24,192 --> 00:00:25,339
Miners provide results.

7
00:00:25,439 --> 00:00:27,000
Validators assess their quality.

8
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:30,323
Bittensor coordinates participation and incentives around that work.

9
00:00:30,423 --> 00:00:40,293
TensorStudio gives the builder a practical starting point: learn the concepts, shape an idea, build an interface, test the scoring and inspect what runs.

10
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:44,031
Let's follow that journey, one step at a time.

11
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,286
New to Bittensor?

12
00:00:51,386 --> 00:00:53,000
Start in Bittensor Quest.

13
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:58,999
Enter the pixel world, move with the arrow keys, and open the learning panel.

14
00:00:59,099 --> 00:01:04,570
Choose TAO Fundamentals, read the short lesson, select an answer and check it.

15
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:08,855
A correct response completes the lesson and adds learning experience.

16
00:01:08,955 --> 00:01:10,998
Next, open the code challenge.

17
00:01:11,098 --> 00:01:16,568
Write the small classroom function, run the local tests and inspect the results.

18
00:01:16,668 --> 00:01:19,140
Progress keeps your next lesson visible.

19
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:25,139
These exercises teach the concepts without sending transactions; learning experience is not real TAO.

20
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:31,614
Now open TensorStudio's Playground.

21
00:01:31,714 --> 00:01:35,648
Ask how to route support messages into billing or technical.

22
00:01:35,748 --> 00:01:40,489
Review the proposed task and next steps, then carry the idea forward.

23
00:01:40,589 --> 00:01:44,523
In Subnet builder, inspect the generated interface beside its code.

24
00:01:44,623 --> 00:01:50,574
Here we add a message about a duplicate charge and mark it for billing review.

25
00:01:50,674 --> 00:01:52,995
Another message describes a login failure.

26
00:01:53,095 --> 00:02:00,660
This is a working human review prototype around the service, keeping the interface separate from the model's actual inference.

27
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:06,766
Next, open Subnet setup.

28
00:02:06,866 --> 00:02:13,389
Describe the classification task and supply labelled examples: a message paired with its expected category.

29
00:02:13,489 --> 00:02:19,571
Generate a recipe and inspect the choices for task, ground truth, scoring and weights.

30
00:02:19,671 --> 00:02:24,428
Assemble the project to create reviewable files and check the code.

31
00:02:24,528 --> 00:02:26,635
Then run the offline harness.

32
00:02:26,735 --> 00:02:30,609
Its report compares correct, incorrect, late and empty answers.

33
00:02:30,709 --> 00:02:35,025
Identical classification labels are not treated as proof of copying.

34
00:02:35,125 --> 00:02:38,998
The calculations are real; the timing values are simulated.

35
00:02:39,098 --> 00:02:43,855
You can see what the scoring rewards before evaluating a network.

36
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:49,485
Open Nodes and inspect the miner started from our assembled project.

37
00:02:49,585 --> 00:02:53,155
Read its logs, stop it, and start it again.

38
00:02:53,255 --> 00:02:58,456
In the console, send a signed test message about a frozen login screen.

39
00:02:58,556 --> 00:03:02,533
The miner returns technical, and that real result streams back.

40
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:08,242
This is a development request, not a chain transaction; registration and rewards remain separate.

41
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:18,623
TensorScan connects the project to observable Bittensor activity.

42
00:03:18,723 --> 00:03:24,962
Follow decoded chain events, then open a subnet dashboard to inspect its registered participants.

43
00:03:25,062 --> 00:03:31,755
These are public testnet observations, not a claim that our development process owns that subnet.

44
00:03:31,855 --> 00:03:39,000
Wallet tools keep identity in the same workspace, while signing material stays encrypted in the browser.

45
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:44,884
Meet TensorNode, the separate compute workspace targeting Lium, subnet fifty one.

46
00:03:44,984 --> 00:03:48,879
First, create a draft with a zero spending limit.

47
00:03:48,979 --> 00:03:52,431
This records intended work without allocating a GPU.

48
00:03:52,531 --> 00:03:58,202
Refresh the workspace and find the new record, read back from the database.

49
00:03:58,302 --> 00:04:02,642
Open its stored event history, then follow the event stream.

50
00:04:02,742 --> 00:04:08,413
Read billing separately from deployment status: an unallocated draft has no rental charge.

51
00:04:08,513 --> 00:04:13,297
Finally, cancel that draft and refresh to verify its saved state.

52
00:04:13,397 --> 00:04:17,292
This existing application recording shows the real control workflow.

53
00:04:17,392 --> 00:04:19,956
Paid GPU execution is not demonstrated.

54
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:29,447
The README maps the whole journey and links every project's code.

55
00:04:29,547 --> 00:04:31,468
Quest is the learning experience.

56
00:04:31,568 --> 00:04:33,085
Studio is the workbench.

57
00:04:33,185 --> 00:04:34,702
TensorScan is the explorer.

58
00:04:34,802 --> 00:04:37,128
TensorNode is the separate operator workspace.

59
00:04:37,228 --> 00:04:44,000
Subnet Factory is also linked as a separate generation prototype, with its integration and network status explained.

60
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:50,688
Start with curiosity, build something small, and inspect the evidence at each step.

61
00:04:50,788 --> 00:04:56,812
Open TensorStudio, follow the beginner's guide, and bring your next Bittensor idea to life.

62
00:04:56,912 --> 00:04:57,688
One idea.

63
00:04:57,788 --> 00:04:59,000
Every next step.
